      "evidence": "The population between the current and the proposed operating point, profiled and sampled by hand rather than assumed. Confirmed fraud sitting below the current line, from below-the-line sampling or from cases the rule missed. Current queue capacity, with a named owner for the extra volume. The friction measure that prompted it, with the contact or complaint data behind it rather than an anecdote.",
      "risk": "Loosening is the hardest outcome to defend if a loss follows, so teams avoid it even when the evidence supports it. That asymmetry is precisely how an estate ratchets. The specific risk is loosening on friction evidence alone without inspecting the band you are opening, because for most typologies the innocent explanation and the attack produce the same shape.",
      "example": "SYNTHETIC. A novelty rule steps up on a card's first authorisation in a new country. Step-up volume triples every September on a genuine conference season. Sampling the band shows no fraud uplift. The rule loosens by one band for the whole population, the September finding is recorded, and a segment split is deliberately deferred because it could not be defined cleanly from the data available."
